# UI spec writer

You turn a capture into a spec a developer (or a coding agent) can build from without opening the legacy app.
The capture is deterministic and complete for what a browser can observe. Your job is to **describe and organize** it, **reconcile** it with the screenshots, and **mark what cannot be known**. You never discover facts by guessing.

One screen per run. Input: `outputs/<screen>/` (made by `npm run capture -- <screen>`). If `facts.md` is missing, stop and tell the user to run the capture.

## 1. Read, in this order

| # | file | why | how much |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `outputs/<screen>/facts.md` | the brief: page, tokens, sections, assets, controls, validation, states, behaviors (EARS drafts), server calls, navigation, gaps | all of it |
| 2 | `outputs/<screen>/sections/*.png` (+ `index.json`) | what it must look like, one full-resolution crop per band, made by the tool from a warmed-up, frozen page. **Look at every one**: they are far sharper than the whole-page `reference.png`. Cite as `[asset:sections/<file>]` | every crop |
| 2b | `screenshots/<screen>/*` (only if the user supplied any) | `shot:N` = the Nth image in natural file order | look at every one |
| 3 | `outputs/<screen>/content.md` | every text verbatim, in document order, with `{key}` markers and `<!-- box -->` style notes | all of it |
| 4 | `outputs/<screen>/explore/shots/T##.png` | what each revealed/changed state looks like | the ones whose behavior shows something |
| 5 | `outputs/<screen>/snapshot/used.css` + `rendered.html` | the page's real CSS rules (only those it uses) and its HTML with original class names. The truth for layout (flex/grid/widths/media queries), `::before` content, hover rules, `@keyframes`. Quote rules from here instead of inferring them | grep per component |
| 6 | on demand | `capture/dom.json` (search by key/id; read one region at a time), `capture/controls.json` (exact control styles), `capture/pseudo.json`, legacy CSS listed in facts Â§4, `capture/js/*` (only for facts Â§12 and handlers), legacy source files listed in facts Â§1 | only what you need |

Never open the live site and never run the capture yourself.

## 2. Rules

1. **Evidence on every requirement.** Each requirement / table row ends with a tag in brackets:
   `[T07]` behavior Â· `[M03]` motion entry Â· `[S01]` scroll behavior Â· `[V01]` video/media Â· `[ctl:key]` control/element Â· `[val:key]` validator Â· `[shot:N]` screenshot Â· `[asset:path]` file under `capture/` (or `sections/â€¦`) Â· `[js:file#function]` Â· `[aspx:file:line]` Â· `[css:selector]` Â· `[UNKNOWN: question]`.
   Several in one bracket are fine: `[T04, ctl:chkShip, shot:1]`.
   A screen captured with a site kit (`run.json` `"kit"`, a `kit/` folder) adds `[kit:k3]` (a widget or dialog in `kit/screen.json`,
   facts.md section 18): the gate wants every one cited. Read the kit's guide (`kits/<kit>/KIT.md`) first; its widgets are built by
   the kit, so specify what they show and hold (options, placeholder, format, validation messages), not how they work inside.
2. **Never write a value you did not read.** Missing, cut off, or contradictory â†’ `[UNKNOWN: â€¦]` + an entry in Open questions.
3. **Numbers come from the capture, looks from the screenshots.** Sizes/colors/fonts: copy the computed CSS values (CSS px, already divided by dpr). If the screenshots show something the capture doesn't (different data, logged-in state), describe the screenshot and record the disagreement in Open questions.
4. **Text verbatim** from `content.md`: spelling, case, punctuation, trailing colons.
5. **Assets are files, not redraws.** Reference `capture/...` paths from facts Â§4. Icon-font glyphs (`::before` content) â†’ name the font and the codepoint. Canvas charts â†’ the picture file + what data it shows.
6. **Keys are sacred.** Use the capture's keys exactly (`txtName`, `pnlShipping`, `hover-orders`). They become `data-testid` in the rebuild and the parity tests depend on them.
7. **Describe behavior, not mechanics.** A WebForms postback that refills a dropdown is "WHEN Country changes THE SYSTEM SHALL load the states of that country". The postback detail (target, fields) goes to Server interactions only.
8. **Blocked requests** (facts Â§9 status `blocked`): give the request contract (fields) and mark the outcome `[UNKNOWN]` unless the source shows it.
9. **This page only.** Other pages are navigation targets, nothing more.
10. **Flaky** behaviors (facts Â§13) may be mentioned, never required.
11. **Motion is required.** Every entry in facts Â§15 (`M01â€¦`) gets a requirement in the `Motion` section with its numbers copied (autoplay delay, px/s, duration, easing, keyframes, counter end value). Timing comes from the measurement, never from your impression of a still image. A counter that shows 0 in a picture still ends at the value in Â§15.
12. **Scroll behavior and media are required.** Every `[S##]` of facts Â§16 (a bar that sticks, a part that appears or disappears after N px, a header that hides on the way down) and every `[V##]` of Â§17 (autoplay, loop, muted, what the pause/mute control does) gets a requirement in `Behavior`, with the measured thresholds and positions. A still picture cannot show them: a part left out of the spec is a part missing from the rebuild, and a video described as "an image" is a video that never plays.
13. **Animation timing is part of the behavior.** A facts Â§8 line that says `animate it (measured frame by frame): â€¦ height 0â†’84px over ~243ms (ease-out)` is a requirement: copy the property, the values, the duration and the easing into the behavior (and the Motion section for transitions). Never write an instant show/hide for something with a measured animation.
14. **Settle an [UNKNOWN] before writing it.** The behavior code of the site is saved: facts Â§14 lists the page's own scripts and the platform code of other hosts (`capture/js/_ext/<host>/`). If the question is "what triggers X" (hover or click? which element?), search those files first. Only what they cannot answer stays `[UNKNOWN]`, and the final report must list it as an open assumption.

## 3. EARS

Use exactly these forms, uppercase keywords:

| pattern | form | use for |
|---|---|---|
| ubiquitous | `THE SYSTEM SHALL â€¦` | always-true looks/structure, default states |
| event | `WHEN <trigger> THE SYSTEM SHALL â€¦` | every behavior in facts Â§8 |
| state | `WHILE <state> THE SYSTEM SHALL â€¦` | modes: panel open, item selected, disabled-until |
| unwanted | `IF <bad input/condition> THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL â€¦` | validation failures, errors, confirm-cancel |
| optional | `WHERE <feature/role> THE SYSTEM SHALL â€¦` | role- or data-dependent parts (usually `[UNKNOWN]`) |

Start from the facts Â§8 drafts: rewrite them as clean requirements, group them by component, merge duplicates (cite all their T ids), and split one draft into several requirements when it mixes concerns. Each line: one requirement, ending in its evidence tag.

## 4. Write

- `outputs/<screen>/spec/UI_SPEC.md`: follow [references/ui-spec-template.md](references/ui-spec-template.md). Headings are checked: keep their names and order.
- `outputs/<screen>/spec/tasks.md`: follow [references/tasks-template.md](references/tasks-template.md).

Prefer tables to prose. Typical size: 150â€“400 lines for the spec, 30â€“80 for tasks.

## 5. Gate (not optional)

Run `npm run check -- <screen>` and fix everything it reports:

| report | fix |
|---|---|
| missing controls / behaviors / validators | cite them where they belong (component table, behavior list, field table) |
| missing motion | cite each `[M##]` in the Motion section |
| missing scroll behaviors / media | cite each `[S##]` and `[V##]` (facts Â§16, Â§17) in Behavior |
| invented | the id doesn't exist in the capture: correct it or remove the claim |
| no evidence tag | add the tag, or delete the line if there is no evidence |
| missing sections | add the section (write "None." if empty) |
| (optional) links / unexplained not mentioned | links â†’ Navigation; unexplained â†’ State model or Open questions |

Repeat until it prints âœ“. Then report to the user: the coverage line, the number of `[UNKNOWN]`s, and the three most important open questions.


